From cd63464763bab9bf90a2a5aa6dedd772ec1704bc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: l4h <z@univalence.me>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 09:29:04 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] x86: optionally copy the console to the boot framebuffer

The fb_console command-line option draws everything the kernel prints,
its own messages and user debug output, on the lower half of the
multiboot2 framebuffer: 8x8 glyphs doubled vertically, wrapping to the
top instead of scrolling, so the framebuffer is never read.  This shows
the console on machines whose serial port cannot be reached, such as
Latitude.sh servers, whose BMC offers only a graphical console through
its web proxy.

Only a direct-RGB, 32 bpp framebuffer between 1 MiB and 4 GiB whose
pitch holds a row of pixels, and that overlaps no usable RAM (checked
once the whole memory map has been read), is used: low memory, which the
memory map leaves out, holds the APs' boot code.  Drawing is clipped to
the console area, even when cores that do not hold the kernel lock
(panics) race on the cursor.
---
